@event fennick_weather_first
# en: Weather First
title [2bac9d] = 先说天气
# en: {&Mrs Fennick, small and brown with forearms like a blacksmith's, greets you with the weather. The wind's gone round west, there's rain by the week's end, and the barley's thinking about it. Then she puts the kettle on and looks at you both. "Well," she says. "There's the roof wants doing, or there's the tea."|Mrs Fennick again, and the weather again: {season == "winter": a frost coming, she can smell it, and the hens know|thunder by evening, and the cows know}. She sets out three cups without asking. "Roof's still wanting," she says, "if you've the arms for it. Or sit."|The kettle's on before you're through the gate. "Wind's in the east," Mrs Fennick says, "and you're thinner," which from her is a whole letter. "Roof," she says, "or tea."}
text [6a82b3] = {&芬尼克太太个子小小、皮肤黝黑，前臂像铁匠一样粗壮，她跟你打招呼先说天气。风转西了，周末前有雨，大麦还在琢磨要不要长。然后她把水壶坐上，看着你们俩。“好了，”她说，“屋顶等着修，茶也等着喝。”|又是芬尼克太太，又是天气：{season == "winter": 要下霜了，她闻得出来，母鸡也知道|傍晚有雷，牛都知道}。她不问一声就摆出三只杯子。“屋顶还等着呢，”她说，“要是你有力气的话。或者坐着。”|你还没进院门，水壶就已经坐上了。“风在东边，”芬尼克太太说，“你瘦了。”从她嘴里说出来，这就是一整封信。“屋顶，”她说，“还是茶。”}
# en: A low stone farmhouse kitchen with a range and a scrubbed table, a small sturdy woman pouring tea, a big gentle young man ducking under the beams, a ladder at the open door.
scene [62f589] = 一间低矮的石头农舍厨房，有炉灶和擦得发白的桌子，一个矮壮的妇人在倒茶，一个魁梧温和的年轻人低头避着房梁，敞开的门口靠着一架梯子。
# en: Help Tobias with the roof
opt.left.label [dfff39] = 帮托比亚斯修屋顶
# en: {&You pass slates up the ladder for an afternoon while Tobias sings to them, which he says helps them sit. Your shoulders burn. Mrs Fennick calls the weather up at you every half hour, and she's right every time.|Up the ladder. Tobias fixes slates and tells you a long story about a goat that ended up on this very roof. Your hands are scraped raw by the end, and the story still isn't over.|You patch the corner where the wind got under the slates, until dusk and until your arms give out. Mrs Fennick stands in the yard with her hands on her hips and says, "That'll keep the weather out," which is the highest thing she says about anything.}
opt.left.say.1 [9abf56] = {&你一下午都在把石板递上梯子，托比亚斯对着石板唱歌，说这样它们能铺得稳。你的肩膀火辣辣的。芬尼克太太每半个钟头朝上面喊一次天气，每次都说中了。|上了梯子。托比亚斯一边铺石板，一边给你讲一只山羊爬上这个屋顶的长故事。到最后你的手都磨破了，故事还没讲完。|你修补风钻进石板底下的那个角，一直修到天黑、胳膊再也抬不起来。芬尼克太太双手叉腰站在院子里说：“这下能把天气挡在外头了。”这是她对任何东西的最高评价。}
# en: Sit and have the tea
opt.right.label [db5e61] = 坐下喝茶
# en: {&Strong tea, seed cake and Mrs Fennick's opinions of the weather for an hour. At the door, as you go, she takes your hand and says, quite briskly, that she's glad Tobias has you. Tobias goes red to the ears and studies the hens.|Tea, and a slab of bread and dripping, and the story of the winter Tobias tried to keep a lamb in his bed. He hides his face in his hands. His mother laughs from the belly, exactly like him.|Tea, and the last of the seed cake, which she has plainly saved. She asks about the castle and listens to every word, and at the end says, "Rain tomorrow," and pats your hand twice.}
opt.right.say.1 [69f428] = {&浓茶、香籽蛋糕，外加芬尼克太太对天气的一个钟头的见解。你走的时候，她在门口握住你的手，干脆利落地说，很高兴托比亚斯有你这个朋友。托比亚斯的耳朵红透了，盯着母鸡看。|茶，一大块抹了油脂的面包，还有托比亚斯小时候想把一只小羊藏在床上过冬的故事。他用手捂住了脸。他母亲笑得前仰后合，跟他一模一样。|茶，还有最后一块香籽蛋糕，显然是她特意留的。她问起城堡的事，每个字都认真听，听完说了句“明天有雨”，拍了两下你的手。}

@event fennick_mandrake_bed
# en: Gerald's Relations
title [6e5627] = 杰拉尔德的亲戚们
# en: Behind the farmhouse is a walled bed of mandrakes, two dozen of them, and every one is wearing a knitted hat: stripes, bobbles, one with ear-flaps. "Gerald's relations," Tobias says proudly. "Ma knits for them all winter. They scream less in a hat." One of them, in purple, glares at you. It's time to lift them for the season, and two have outgrown their hats.
text [ae7d92] = 农舍后面有一块围起来的曼德拉草苗圃，二十几株，每一株都戴着一顶毛线帽：条纹的、带绒球的，还有一顶带护耳的。“杰拉尔德的亲戚们，”托比亚斯骄傲地说，“我妈整个冬天都在给它们织帽子。戴着帽子它们叫得少一些。”其中一株戴紫帽子的瞪着你。到了该把它们挖出来换地方的时候，有两株已经长得戴不下帽子了。
# en: A walled garden bed behind a farmhouse, a row of small grumpy mandrakes each wearing a brightly knitted hat, a big young man kneeling beside them with a basket of wool.
scene [32087e] = 农舍后面一块围起来的苗圃，一排小小的、脾气暴躁的曼德拉草，每株都戴着鲜艳的毛线帽，一个魁梧的年轻人提着一篮毛线跪在旁边。
# en: Help lift them
opt.left.label [ee285b] = 帮忙把它们挖出来
# en: You lift them one by one, quickly, each with a thin indignant shriek, and your ears ring all afternoon. Tobias talks to every one by name. The purple one hisses at you. "That's Auntie Maud," he says. "Not that Maud. Well. A bit that Maud."
opt.left.say.1 [6e8ed6] = 你一株一株飞快地把它们挖出来，每一株都发出一声尖细的抗议，你的耳朵嗡嗡响了一下午。托比亚斯叫着每一株的名字跟它们说话。紫帽子那株冲你嘶嘶叫。“那是莫德姨妈，”他说，“不是那个莫德。嗯，有一点像那个莫德。”
# en: Knit the new hats
opt.right.label [cdd576] = 织新帽子
# en: Mrs Fennick sits you down with needles and green wool and no mercy. You drop stitches, swear, drop more, and the hat comes out lopsided and you're cross with it. Tobias puts it on the smallest mandrake, who stops screaming and goes to sleep. "It likes you," he says, delighted, which almost makes up for it.
opt.right.say.1 [e9a7ce] = 芬尼克太太让你坐下，塞给你毛线针和绿毛线，毫不留情。你漏了针，骂了一句，又漏了更多针，帽子织歪了，你对它很是恼火。托比亚斯把它戴在最小的那株曼德拉草头上，它不叫了，睡着了。“它喜欢你，”他高兴地说，这差不多算是补偿了。

@event fennick_good_boots
# en: A Man in Good Boots
title [498711] = 穿好靴子的人
# en: There's a man at the farm gate in good city boots, with a leather folder and a smile he's practised. He's from the capital, he says, and buying upland farms: a fair price, this year only. "Bad years come," he tells Mrs Fennick pleasantly, "and good years don't, up here. You'd be wise." She doesn't move from her step. Tobias has gone very quiet.
text [a56e39] = 农场门口站着一个穿着城里好靴子的男人，拿着一个皮夹子，脸上挂着练过的笑。他说他从王都来，在收购高地上的农场：价钱公道，只限今年。“坏年景会来的，”他客客气气地对芬尼克太太说，“好年景在这上头可不会来。您最好想清楚。”她站在门阶上一动不动。托比亚斯一下子沉默了。
# en: A farm gate on a windy upland, a smartly dressed man from the city with a leather folder, a small woman with her arms folded on the doorstep, a big young man standing very still.
scene [236dac] = 风大的高地上一扇农场大门，一个衣着讲究、拿着皮夹子的城里人，一个双臂抱胸站在门阶上的矮小妇人，一个一动不动的魁梧年轻人。
# en: Tell him to clear off
opt.left.label [baafed] = 叫他走开
# en: "She said no," you say, though she hasn't said anything. The man looks at you, and writes something in the folder, and smiles. "I'll remember you," he says, and goes, and you feel his look on your back all down the lane. Mrs Fennick nods once. Tobias lets out a breath.
opt.left.say.1 [b65a4e] = “她说了不卖，”你说，虽然她什么都没说。那人看了看你，在皮夹子里写了点什么，笑了。“我会记住你的，”他说完就走了，你沿着小路一直感觉到他的目光落在你背上。芬尼克太太点了一下头。托比亚斯松了口气。
# en: Ask who he's buying for
opt.right.label [e6b6ba] = 问他替谁收购
# en: You get him talking about the company he buys for, and the farms he's bought, all on the upland side, all this year. He gives you his card before he realises he's said too much: an office on the capital's uptown side. Mrs Fennick takes the card off you and puts it on the fire.
opt.right.say.1 [c8477c] = 你引着他聊起他替哪家公司收购，聊起他已经买下的那些农场，全在高地这一侧，全是今年买的。他还没意识到自己说多了，就递给了你一张名片：王都上城的一间事务所。芬尼克太太从你手里拿过名片，丢进了炉火。
# en: He smiles and says his principals prefer discretion, and asks your name instead, which you don't give. He tips his hat to Mrs Fennick and goes. Neither Fennick will talk about it at supper, and the silence sits heavy on the table.
opt.right.say.2 [ebd40f] = 他笑着说委托人喜欢低调，反过来问你的名字，你没告诉他。他向芬尼克太太抬了抬帽子，走了。晚饭时芬尼克家谁也不肯提这件事，沉默沉甸甸地压在饭桌上。

@event fennick_top_field
# en: Outside the Luck
title [2a2858] = 运气之外
# en: Tobias walks you up to the top field, where the farm meets the valley's land at an old drystone wall. {season == "winter": On the far side the stubble's long turned in, and every yard below has ricks standing fat and gold. On the Fennick side the ground is stones, and there's one thin rick, and it's honest.|On the far side the barley stands thick and gold. On the Fennick side it's thin, and patchy, and honest.} "Seven good years down there," Tobias says. "Every year. Never up here." He puts his big hand flat on the wall. "Ma says the valley's luck is the valley's business."
text [75f95a] = 托比亚斯带你走到最高的那块田，农场在那里跟山谷的地隔着一道古老的干砌石墙。{season == "winter": 墙那边，麦茬早就翻进了地里，下面家家院子里的草垛都垛得又肥又金黄。芬尼克家这边满地石头，只有一垛瘦瘦的草垛，老老实实。|墙那边，大麦长得又密又金黄。芬尼克家这边，稀稀拉拉，东一块西一块，老老实实。}“下面连着七个好年景，”托比亚斯说，“年年都好。这上头从来没有。”他把大手平放在墙上。“我妈说，山谷的运气是山谷自己的事。”
# en: A high field divided by an old drystone wall, rich land on the far side, thin stony land on the near side, a big young man and a student standing at the wall.
scene [69c685] = 一块被古老干砌石墙隔开的高处田地，墙那边土地肥沃，墙这边贫瘠多石，一个魁梧的年轻人和一个学生站在墙边。
# en: Help him clear the stones
opt.left.label [859624] = 帮他捡石头
# en: You pick stones out of the thin side of the wall until your back aches and the sun goes down. It's slow, plain, endless work, and Tobias hums the whole time. "Our luck's this," he says, tipping a basket. "Stones. I don't mind it. It's ours."
opt.left.say.1 [5c882f] = 你从墙这边的薄地里捡石头，一直捡到腰酸背疼、太阳下山。这活儿又慢又单调，没完没了，托比亚斯一直在哼歌。“我们的运气就是这个，”他倒掉一筐石头说，“石头。我不在乎。这是我们自己的。”
# en: Ask what he thinks it is
opt.right.label [08f75f] = 问他觉得那是什么
# en: He thinks about it a long time, which is how he thinks about everything. "I think they pay for it," he says at last. "I don't know what with. Nobody down there ever says." {season == "winter": The wind goes over the valley's fields below and lifts nothing, not a straw, and comes up the hill to you cold.|The wind goes over the barley on the far side, and none of it bends the way the near side does.} You walk back uneasy.
opt.right.say.1 [798eb2] = 他想了很久，他想什么都是这样。“我觉得他们是付了代价的，”他终于说，“我不知道拿什么付的。下面从来没人说。”{season == "winter": 风从下面山谷的田地上吹过，一根麦秆也没吹动，冷冷地吹上山来到你身边。|风吹过墙那边的大麦，那边的麦子没有一株像这边的一样弯腰。}你们走回去时，你心里很不安。

@event almanac_type_case
# en: Backwards and Upside Down
title [f9c642] = 反着来，倒着来
# en: {&At her mother's type case Nessa is a different creature, silent and quick, setting metal letters into a stick backwards and upside down, faster than she writes forwards. Mrs Quill works the press behind her, ink to the elbows. "Took her apart at five," Mrs Quill says, "the case. Put it back alphabetical. Took me a month."|Nessa's at the case again, setting the market notices, and she hasn't said a single headline since you came in. It's unnerving. "Here," she says, without looking up, and hands you a stick.|The shop again, and the press thumping. Nessa has set your name in type and left it on the stone where you'll see it, which she pretends was an accident. "Line's yours," she says. "If you can."}
text [671e03] = {&在她母亲的字盘前，内莎完全变了个人，安静又利落，把金属铅字反着、倒着排进排字盘，比她正着写字还快。奎尔太太在她身后操作印刷机，墨一直沾到手肘。“她五岁就把字盘拆了，”奎尔太太说，“然后按字母顺序装了回去。害我花了一个月。”|内莎又在字盘前排集市告示，你进来以后她一句标题都没说过。真让人心里发毛。“喏，”她头也不抬地递给你一个排字盘。|又是这间铺子，印刷机咚咚作响。内莎用铅字排好了你的名字，放在你一定会看见的石台上，还假装是不小心。“这一行归你，”她说，“要是你会的话。”}
# en: A crowded country print shop, a young woman in ink-stained sleeves setting metal type into a stick at a tall wooden case, an older woman working a hand press behind her.
scene [c44b4a] = 一间拥挤的乡村印刷铺，一个袖子沾满墨迹的年轻女子在高高的木字架前把金属铅字排进排字盘，身后一个年长的女人在操作手摇印刷机。
# en: Set a line yourself
opt.left.label [1c7f5b] = 自己排一行
# en: {&You set one line in the time she sets a page, and spell it wrong backwards. She corrects it without comment, which is worse. By the end your fingertips are black and your pride is small.|You set the price of eggs and the date of the fair, and get the fair wrong. Nessa fixes it with one flick and says nothing, kindly, which is maddening.|You set a whole notice, slowly, under her eye, and get one letter upside down. She turns it without a word. It stings, a little, that she saw it first.}
opt.left.say.1 [77a7ea] = {&她排一页的工夫，你才排了一行，还反着拼错了。她一声不吭地改好，这更糟。到最后你的指尖全黑了，自尊心也缩成了一小团。|你排了鸡蛋的价钱和集市的日子，把日子排错了。内莎一拨就改好，好心地什么也没说，这更叫人恼火。|你在她的注视下慢慢排完一整条告示，有一个字母排倒了。她一声不响地把它转过来。她先看出来了，这让你有点不是滋味。}
# en: Pull the press for Mrs Quill
opt.right.label [e44e34] = 替奎尔太太拉印刷机
# en: {&Mrs Quill lets you pull the press. A heave, a thump, a sheet off the stone, again, and again, a hundred times. Your arms are dead by the end. "Same as she did at ten," says Mrs Quill. "She cried. You're doing better."|You pull the almanac covers, a thump to each. Your shoulder aches. Mrs Quill talks the whole time about Nessa as a child, and Nessa, at the case, sets faster and faster.|You and Mrs Quill pull the funeral cards for a farmer you never met. It's slow, careful, heavy work, and quiet. "He'd have liked it done properly," she says, and you ache, and are glad you did.}
opt.right.say.1 [1b1eb7] = {&奎尔太太让你拉印刷机。一拉，一压，从石台上揭下一张纸，再来，再来，上百遍。到最后你的胳膊都没知觉了。“她十岁时也这么干，”奎尔太太说，“她哭了。你比她强。”|你印历书的封面，每张都咚地一压。你的肩膀酸了。奎尔太太一直在讲内莎小时候的事，内莎在字盘前排得越来越快。|你和奎尔太太一起印一位你从没见过的农夫的讣闻卡片。这活儿又慢又仔细，又重，又安静。“他会希望好好办的，”她说，你浑身酸疼，却庆幸自己帮了忙。}

@event almanac_first_notice
# en: Lost: One Brown Dog
title [c725ad] = 寻：一只棕色的狗
# en: In the corner of the shop window, yellow with sun, is an old notice: LOST, one brown dog, answers to Biscuit, last seen being a nuisance on the green. The type is crooked and the spelling is fierce. "Don't," Nessa says. "I was seven. It was my first story." She pauses. "He came home. The story worked."
text [bf37ec] = 铺子橱窗的角落里，有一张晒得发黄的旧告示：寻狗，棕色，名叫饼干，最后一次见到时正在草地上捣乱。铅字排得歪歪扭扭，拼写却气势汹汹。“别看，”内莎说，“我那时七岁。那是我的第一篇报道。”她顿了顿。“它回家了。报道起作用了。”
# en: A faded old printed notice pinned in the corner of a print-shop window, LOST in large letters above a woodcut of a dog, a young woman beside it looking embarrassed.
scene [43247c] = 印刷铺橱窗角落里钉着一张褪色的旧印刷告示，大大的“寻”字下面是一幅狗的木刻画，一个年轻女子站在旁边，一脸难为情。
# en: Ask her to tell it properly
opt.left.label [6dac2f] = 请她好好讲讲
# en: She tells it like a front page: the disappearance, the search, the witnesses, the triumphant return of Biscuit, muddy, with a sausage. She was insufferable for a year, she says. "I still am," she adds. "But now I know it's the job."
opt.left.say.1 [2fbc13] = 她讲得像头版新闻：失踪、搜寻、目击者、饼干凯旋归来，浑身是泥，嘴里叼着一根香肠。她说她得意了整整一年。“现在还是，”她补充道，“不过现在我知道这就是干这行的。”
# en: Write her a new notice
opt.right.label [40ed82] = 给她写一张新告示
# en: You write a notice for the window: FOUND, one editor, answers to Nessa, last seen being a nuisance in the castle. She edits it, ruthlessly, three times, until hardly a word of yours is left, and you sulk a little. Then she sets it, prints it, and pins it beside Biscuit.
opt.right.say.1 [aaa8c1] = 你给橱窗写了一张告示：寻获，编辑一名，名叫内莎，最后一次见到时正在城堡里捣乱。她毫不留情地改了三遍，改到你写的字几乎一个不剩，你有点赌气。然后她把它排好，印出来，钉在了饼干旁边。

@event almanac_short_page
# en: A Page Short
title [33efcd] = 少了一页
# en: In the attic, a century of the valley's almanacs sit in bundles under the eaves, and Nessa has been counting pages. "Every seventh year it's a page short," she says. "Always the harvest page. Mother says the old press did that." She holds two copies side by side. "It's not the press." {seen(market_harvest_board) > 0: You think of the harvest board's columns of seven.|}
text [3ba8e1] = 阁楼的屋檐下一捆捆堆着山谷一百年的历书，内莎一直在数页数。“每七年就少一页，”她说，“总是收成那一页。母亲说老印刷机就这样。”她把两本并排举起来。“不是印刷机的问题。”{seen(market_harvest_board) > 0: 你想起了那块收成木板上七个一列的格子。|}
# en: A low attic under the eaves stacked with bundles of old thin almanacs, a young woman with a lantern turning pages, dust in the air.
scene [9c406c] = 屋檐下一间低矮的阁楼，堆满一捆捆薄薄的旧历书，一个年轻女子提着灯翻着书页，空气里飘着灰尘。
# en: Go through them with her
opt.left.label [37fe33] = 跟她一起翻
# en: You go through the bundles until the lantern gutters, coughing in the dust, and she's right: seven, fourteen, twenty-one, always the harvest page, always cut clean. Downstairs, Mrs Quill calls up that supper's on, a little too brightly. Nessa writes it all down, and for once says nothing.
opt.left.say.1 [8f8ed6] = 你们在灰尘里咳嗽着，一捆一捆地翻，直到灯芯快灭了，她说得没错：第七年、第十四年、第二十一年，总是收成那一页，总是裁得干干净净。楼下，奎尔太太喊你们下来吃晚饭，声音有点过于轻快。内莎把一切都记了下来，难得一句话也没说。
# en: Tell her to let it lie
opt.right.label [25e63e] = 劝她别管了
# en: "Some things are just the press," you say. Nessa looks at you over the lantern for a long moment, the way she looks at someone who's said something quotable. "That's what Mother says," she says, and puts the almanacs back, and doesn't believe either of you.
opt.right.say.1 [abe21d] = “有些事就是印刷机的问题，”你说。内莎隔着灯看了你很久，就像她看一个说了句值得引用的话的人。“母亲也这么说，”她说着把历书放了回去，你们俩的话她一个都不信。

@event tobias_country_barrow
# en: Turnips, Mostly Free
title [b767f2] = 芜菁，基本免费
# en: Tobias comes down the lane with a barrow of turnips he's meant to be selling, and he's selling them terribly. Anyone with a sad story gets one free, and in this valley everyone has a sad story. "Ma'll kill me," he says cheerfully, handing one to a man whose cow is poorly. "Help?"
text [a98897] = 托比亚斯推着一车芜菁从小路上走来，本该拿去卖的，可他卖得一塌糊涂。谁有伤心事就白送一个，而在这个山谷里，人人都有伤心事。“我妈会宰了我的，”他乐呵呵地说，把一个递给一位家里牛病了的男人，“帮个忙？”
# en: A country lane, a big gentle young man pushing a barrow heaped with turnips, a small crowd of villagers each holding a turnip, a student watching.
scene [d8fc87] = 一条乡间小路，一个魁梧温和的年轻人推着堆满芜菁的独轮车，一小群村民每人手里拿着一个芜菁，一个学生在一旁看着。
# en: Run the barrow properly
opt.left.label [61db65] = 好好地把车上的卖掉
# en: You take over: a fair price, no stories. You sell the lot before long and hand Tobias the takings. He looks at the coins as though they were slightly sad, and pays you your share, and is a little quiet with you on the way home.
opt.left.say.1 [ee0698] = 你接手了：价钱公道，不听故事。没多久你就卖光了，把钱交给托比亚斯。他看着那些钱币，好像它们有点可怜，分了你一份，回去的路上对你有点沉默。
# en: Give them away with him
opt.right.label [a02a12] = 跟他一起送掉
# en: You hand out turnips to a widow, a tinker, two children's grandmothers and a goat that asks nicely. Your pockets stay empty and your face aches from smiling. "Ma'll kill us both," Tobias says, very happy. "Worth it."
opt.right.say.1 [892ba4] = 你把芜菁分给了一个寡妇、一个补锅匠、两位孩子的奶奶，还有一只很有礼貌地讨要的山羊。你的口袋空空，脸都笑酸了。“我妈会把我们俩都宰了，”托比亚斯说，高兴极了，“值。”

@event nessa_country_green
# en: Nothing Ever Happens Here
title [4ddfb7] = 这儿什么都不会发生
# en: Nessa is sitting on a bench by the green with her notebook shut, which you've never seen. The village is having a perfect afternoon around her. "I left," she says, "because nothing here was ever news. Seven good harvests and everyone smiling, and nobody ever asked why." She opens the notebook, and closes it again. "I'm starting to think that was the news."
text [4915af] = 内莎坐在草地边的长椅上，笔记本合着，这你从来没见过。村子在她周围过着完美的下午。“我离开，”她说，“是因为这里从来没有新闻。连着七个好收成，人人都笑眯眯的，从来没人问为什么。”她打开笔记本，又合上。“我开始觉得，那本身就是新闻。”
# en: A quiet village green in the long afternoon, a young woman sitting on a bench with a notebook shut on her knee, a student beside her, thatched cottages and a market cross behind.
scene [578eb5] = 漫长午后一片安静的村庄草地，一个年轻女子坐在长椅上，笔记本合着放在膝头，一个学生坐在她旁边，身后是茅草农舍和集市十字架。
# en: Say maybe it's a story
opt.left.label [f6637d] = 说也许这就是个故事
# en: "Maybe it is," you say. She looks at you as if you'd handed her a key, and then, carefully, as if it might be a trap. "Don't encourage me," she says, and opens the notebook, and writes a single word you can't read. The village goes on smiling. You find you don't like it.
opt.left.say.1 [e4fd16] = “也许是的，”你说。她看着你，像你递给了她一把钥匙，然后又小心翼翼地看着你，好像那可能是个陷阱。“别怂恿我，”她说，打开笔记本，写下一个你看不清的词。村子继续笑眯眯的。你发现自己不喜欢这样。
# en: Tell her she can be bored here
opt.right.label [42c9c6] = 说她可以在这儿无聊一下
# en: "Be bored," you say. "With me. For one afternoon." She tries. She manages almost an hour, which is a record, watching ducks and saying nothing quotable at all. Then she sees the miller across the green and is off, notebook open, and you laugh.
opt.right.say.1 [96e3e9] = “无聊一下吧，”你说，“跟我一起。就一个下午。”她试了。她坚持了将近一个钟头，看鸭子，一句值得引用的话都没说，这是纪录。然后她看见草地那头的磨坊主，笔记本一打开就冲了过去，你笑了。
